  "primary_inspection": "Original ST printed p. 278 and Boalch printed p. 1 were extracted, rendered and visually read. ST Table VII and complete exception footnote were already directly read and visually inspected in the global audit. Official ATLAS page was browsed and retained verbatim. No isomorphism is inferred merely from a group order.",
  "written_arguments": {
    "determinant_cover": "Involutory reflection generators force determinant values +/-1. Since -I is in the scalar kernel and has determinant -1 in dimension three, H=ker(det) maps onto the projective quotient. Its scalar kernel is trivial for G24 and C3 for G27.",
    "unique_involutions": "In a central odd-order kernel A, an arbitrary lift h of a projective involution has h^2 in A; the bijective square map supplies a unique a with a^2=h^-2. Thus ah is the unique involutory lift. Uniqueness is conjugation-equivariant.",
    "Wirtinger_lift": "Assign each arc meridian its unique involutory H lift. Every signed Wirtinger conjugation relation compares two involutions above the same projective element, so holds exactly. This is a knot-presentation argument, not an arbitrary normal-generation lifting assertion.",
    "parity_twist": "Multiply by (-I)^ell(g), with ell the knot abelianization. A nontrivial determinant-one involution in dimension three has spectrum (1,-1,-1); the meridian twist has spectrum (-1,1,1).",
    "one_reflection_class": "det(W)={+/-1} makes every reflection involutory. It has a unique lift above each projective involution, namely -t(q). A6 double transpositions form one class; GL3(F2) nontrivial involutions all have Jordan type (2,1). Hence every reflection of W is in the same class.",
    "full_image_and_irreducibility": "For J=psi(knot group), projective surjectivity gives W=JZ. The W-conjugacy class of a meridian reflection is its J-conjugacy class and lies in J. It is every reflection, which generate W. Thus J=W, and natural irreducibility is imported from ST.",
    "class_rank_three": "ST gives three reflection generators. Conversely their rank-one displacement images span a nonzero invariant subspace; irreducibility forces dimension three, so at least three generators are needed.",
    "sixteen_exact_capacities": "Existing all-parameter rank-one irreducible-dimension and augmentation uppers for the exact sixteen diagrams, together with the finite three-dimensional positives and global finite-class domination, give finite class capacity = finite KCH capacity = ordinary complex augmentation rank = 3."
  },
